FlashFill:
submitted by Javier Segovia (Universitat Pompeu Fabra) <javier.segovia@upf.edu>

FlashFill models the FlashFill feature of Excel that synthesizes programs from
input/output examples. The inputs are strings that must be transformed into
other strings (e.g., extract the capital letters of the name and surname) and
the output is a program that works on all examples. This can be encoded as a
classical planning problem using the planning programs compilation by Segovia
et al.

Reference: Javier Segovia-Aguas, Sergio Jiménez, Anders Jonsson. Generalized
Planning with Procedural Domain Control Knowledge. ICAPS 2016.


Generator:

The generator consists of two steps. First, generate-example.py generates
random examples for one of the following tasks:

  add-paren SIZE:                   (BOB => (BOB)
  extract-minutes:                  8:23.65 => 23
  reverse NAME SURNAME:             BOB DOE => DOE BOB
  first-last-initial NAME SURNAME:  BOB DOE => BOB D
  initials NAME SURNAME:            BOB DOE => B D

Then generate.py compiles the domain domain_variables.pddl and the examples
into a planning task (its own domain and problem file) whose solutions are
programs with the given number of lines. It uses the C++ compiler in src/,
which "make" builds. Example:

    make
    ./generate-example.py initials 3 4 --seed 1 > example1.pddl
    ./generate-example.py initials 4 3 --seed 2 > example2.pddl
    ./generate.py 4 example1.pddl example2.pddl --domain domain.pddl --problem problem.pddl


Changes for pddl-generators:

- Build the compiler with a Makefile instead of SCons and remove the parts
  that the compiler does not need.
- Merge the Python 2 scripts gen01.py, ..., gen05.py that generated the
  examples into the Python 3 script generate-example.py, which uses a given
  random seed. The original scripts wrote the time of creation into the
  problem names.
- Move the post-processing of the generated domains (only declare the used
  characters and add missing requirements) from shell scripts into
  generate.py.
- generate-ipc-instances.py generates the 36 candidate tasks for IPC 2018 from
  the examples in examples/. They are identical to the tasks generated by the
  original generator, except for whitespace. The examples of the 20 tasks that
  were finally used in IPC 2018 are not part of the original repository.
